  "why": "The statement names a family, so no single event can be whole; everything settling one named conjecture or a restricted class is a case. I read the family as the homological conjectures of commutative algebra: the article's fourteen items plus the commutative Peskine–Szpiro, Huneke–Şega–Vraciu, Huneke–Wiegand and commutative Auslander–Reiten conjectures, which belong to the same literature. Artin-algebra (noncommutative) results, the answered questions that fall short of the conjectures, and neighbouring conjectures are variant. Equivalence theorems and neighbour papers are none. Ma's dimension-inequality counterexample is credited by the author's own disclosure to ChatGPT, so generated. Codex is credited with the CGI construction and the proof, so generated. Böhmler–Marczinzik ('found with the assistance') and Pham ('assisted with the search') are assisted. Pham counts as digested because Huneke, named, independently recomputed the example, and CGI corroborate it. Böhmler–Marczinzik's 5-dimensional algebra is directly checkable. The OpenAI 193 Serre-positivity claim is a case (only the positivity half of item 13) and doubtful, because of the specific gaps the record names. OpenAI 195 refutes item 14 and is a case, unreviewed; its Lean check covers only the ring's properties.",
  "doubts": "Family scope is the main judgement call: if only the article's 14 named items count, then Ma, Böhmler–Marczinzik, Pham, CGI, Christensen–Nasseh and Lyle would be variant, and only KC–Soto Levins and OpenAI 193/195 would remain. Pham's repository is undated, but it was cited by 21 Aug 2026, so it falls after the snapshot either way. Huneke's verification note itself was not read. The CGI counterexample and proof have an unresolved consistency question. The Ma dimension counterexample is AI-found, but the grade counterexample is the author's own; I rated the bundled event by its strongest contribution.",
